OC-OPD: Observation-coordinated Supervision for On-Policy Distillation Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2611.01965v1 [cs.LG] 01 Nov 2026 OC-OPD: Observation-coordinated Supervision for On-Policy Distillation Ruoran Xu Yilun Zhao Ziyu Chen Arman Cohan Affiliation: Yale NLP Lab Abstract Reverse and forward Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergences provide complementary supervision in on-policy distillation: reverse KL refines the student’s active token rankings, and forward KL allocates mass to teacher-supported candidates. At student-generated prefixes, we form a four-observation matrix by treating the student and teacher as both candidate sources and evaluators. The diagonal self-observations measure uncertainty, while source-centered cross-observations recover the two KL objectives. We establish an exact identity equating the Fisher inverse-metric coupling of their logit gradients with the sum of these contrasts, yielding a common local descent direction for distinct positive distributions. Observation-coordinated on-policy distillation (OC-OPD) uses observations to set preferences, closed-form bargaining to coordinate gradients, and distributional discrepancy to scale the update. Across three heterogeneous student settings (non-thinking generation, thinking generation, and base-model initialization), OC-OPD achieves strong gains in mathematical reasoning and transfers effectively to out-of-distribution scientific question answering and code generation. Our code is publicly available at: https://github.com/RuoranXu/OC-OPD. Figure 1: Training pipeline of OC-OPD. (a) Student rollouts provide prefixes at which the student and frozen teacher produce aligned next-token distributions. (b) Four self- and cross-observations at the shared prefix yield source-centered KL gradients for ranking/refinement and coverage. (c) Self-observation values set uncertainty-based priorities for closed-form gradient bargaining, while distributional discrepancy controls supervision strength. (d) The resulting bounded logit signal updates the student through a single backward pass. The updated student then generates new rollouts. 1 Introduction Earlier knowledge distillation methods use KL divergences to transfer predictive structure from a teacher to a student ( Hinton et al., 2015 ; Kim and Rush, 2016 ) . Adaptive KL (AKL) adjusts the early-training balance between forward and reverse KL according to distributional gaps within teacher-defined high-probability head and long-tail regions ( Wu et al., 2025 ) . On-policy distillation (OPD) trains a student at its own generated prefixes, where a frozen teacher provides supervision for the same next-token decision ( Ross et al., 2011 ; Agarwal et al., 2024b ) . At each prefix, the aligned student distribution q q and teacher distribution p p define two complementary objectives. Reverse KL (RKL), D KL ( q ∥ p ) D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\|p) , is associated with mode-seeking refinement of student-supported candidates, while forward KL (FKL), D KL ( p ∥ q ) D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p\|q) , provides mode-covering supervision toward teacher-supported alternatives ( Agarwal et al., 2024b ; Gu et al., 2024 ) . As the student changes, so do the visited prefixes and the conditional predictions of both models. How should these corrections be coordinated within one update at the current teacher–student state? Recent OPD methods adapt supervision using uncertainty, token importance, teacher–student mismatch, and divergence routing ( Ko et al., 2026 ; Jin et al., 2026 ; Lee et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026 ; Yu et al., 2026 ) . At a visited prefix, two aspects of the predictive state inform this adaptation: each model’s self-uncertainty and the disagreement between their predictions. Self-uncertainty describes the dispersion of a model’s candidate probabilities; disagreement describes the mismatch requiring correction. Connecting these quantities to the two KL gradients provides a state-dependent basis for their relative influence and joint improvement. We place these signals in a common source–evaluator system: the student and teacher each supply candidate mass, and either model can evaluate it. The resulting four-observation matrix separates self-uncertainty, represented by its diagonal entropies, from directed disagreement, recovered by centering its cross-entropies on the corresponding source entropies. These contrasts yield the refinement and coverage objectives at the same student-visited state. This representation also links observations to correction geometry. For positive p , q p,q , the reverse- and forward-KL logit gradients satisfy g R ⊤ J ( q ) + g F = D KL ( q ∥ p ) + D KL ( p ∥ q ) g_{R}^{	op}J(q)^{+}g_{F}=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\|p)+D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p\|q) , where J ⁡ ( q ) J(q) is the categorical Fisher matrix. For p ≠ q p
eq q , this positive cross-observation excess establishes a common local descent direction for both KL objectives in student-logit space. We develop observation-coordinated on-policy distillation (OC-OPD) by connecting the observed teaching state to three decisions: preference , direction , and strength . Self-entropies set preferences between the two corrections, a closed-form bargaining solution coordinates their normalized gradients within the common-descent region, and symmetric distributional discrepancy scales supervision strength. These components connect self-uncertainty, correction geometry, and disagreement in a single bounded logit update, applied with one student backward pass. Figure 1 summarizes the observation → ightarrow contrast → ightarrow coordination → ightarrow strength pipeline. We evaluate OC-OPD on mathematical reasoning in three Qwen3 student settings ( Qwen Team, 2025c ) , all trained with fresh student rollouts: non-thinking students at 1.7B and 4B scales, thinking-generation students, and a weaker base-model initialization. In the main non-thinking setting, the 1.7B student exceeds the strongest compared baseline by + 6.88 +6.88 Avg@32 and + 11.11 +11.11 Pass@32 points on average across AIME24–26; the 4B student exceeds EOPD ( Jin et al., 2026 ) by + 2.74 +2.74 and + 3.33 +3.33 points, respectively (Table 1 ). Gains persist in the other two settings, and GPQA-Diamond ( Rein et al., 2023 ) and MBPP+ ( Liu et al., 2023 ) assess transfer to scientific question answering and code generation. Our contributions are threefold. (1) We unifiedly describe token-level OPD through a source–evaluator system whose self- and cross-observations characterize the teaching state and whose source-centered contrasts recover the two KL corrections. (2) We prove a Fisher inverse-metric identity linking those contrasts to their logit gradients and establish common local descent for every distinct positive teacher–student pair. (3) We develop OC-OPD to determine the preference, direction, and strength of token-level supervision, and demonstrate its effectiveness across heterogeneous student settings and out-of-domain evaluations. 2 Related Work KL directions in on-policy distillation. Earlier knowledge distillation methods transfer predictive structure from a teacher to a student ( Hinton et al., 2015 ; Kim and Rush, 2016 ) . AKL adaptively combines forward and reverse KL using distributional gaps within teacher-defined head and tail regions, motivated by their different fitting priorities in early training ( Wu et al., 2025 ) . A larger head gap increases the forward-KL weight, while a larger tail gap increases the reverse-KL weight. OPD evaluates teacher guidance at student-generated prefixes ( Ross et al., 2011 ; Agarwal et al., 2024b ) , where both models produce aligned next-token distributions. GKD studies flexible divergence objectives at these states ( Agarwal et al., 2024b ) , and MiniLLM develops reverse-KL distillation under student sampling ( Gu et al., 2024 ) . Related methods explore skewed divergences and adaptive sampling ( Ko et al., 2024 ) , contrastive teacher–student responses ( Ko et al., 2025 ) , and interleaved generation ( Xu et al., 2025 ) . Reverse KL refines relative probabilities among student-supported candidates, while forward KL transfers mass toward teacher-supported candidates. EOPD conditions forward-KL supervision on teacher entropy ( Jin et al., 2026 ) , and SEAD adjusts the emphasis of the two KL directions across tokens ( Lee et al., 2026 ) . We study how the current teacher–student state relates to these corrections: source-centering separates self-uncertainty from directed disagreement, and an exact observation–gradient identity establishes their joint local compatibility. Observation-based adaptive supervision. At a shared prefix, either model can supply candidate mass and either model can evaluate it. These choices produce four observations: two self-entropies describe the predictive states of the student and teacher, and two cross-entropies combine source uncertainty with directed cross-model disagreement. Related methods use local information to determine how supervision is assigned. REOPOLD uses entropy-based token selection and relaxed reward shaping ( Ko et al., 2026 ) ; SMOPD selects informative positions for self-distillation ( Jiang and Huang, 2026 ) ; TIP combines student uncertainty with divergence to assess token importance ( Xu et al., 2026 ) , while TIDE characterizes student-excess and student-deficit mismatch ( Yu et al., 2026 ) . Region-dependent objectives, adaptive targets, bounded transformations, and trust-region control further shape local supervision ( Jia et al., 2026 ; Jang et al., 2026 ; Zhao et al., 2026a ; Xing et al., 2026 ) . Our source–evaluator matrix connects these signals through the entropy decomposition of KL: source-centering isolates the two directed disagreements, whose gradients supply the refinement and coverage corrections. Self-entropies set their relative preferences, gradient geometry coordinates their direction, and distributional discrepancy sets supervision strength. Appendix G summarizes representative methods in this notation. Gradient coordination. Multi-objective optimization derives shared update directions via minimum-norm descent, gradient projection, conflict-averse optimization, and bargaining. Our KL gradients stem from aligned student–teacher distributions and source-centered observation contrasts, whose local coupling follows an exact Fisher-geometric identity. We leverage this identity to coordinate candidate-level refinement and coverage, adopting bargaining to obtain an observation-conditioned closed-form update direction. Scalarization and normalized gradient mixing serve as baselines for comparison. 3 Preliminaries and Motivation This section formalizes shared-context OPD, organizes the aligned student and teacher distributions into four observations, and shows how their source-centered contrasts recover the ranking and coverage signals of reverse and forward KL. 3.1 Shared-context OPD as observation system OPD evaluates supervision along student-generated responses by querying the teacher at the same prefixes ( Agarwal et al., 2024b ; Gu et al., 2024 ) . We distinguish this choice of training states from the information available at each visited state. In the shared-context setting, both models condition on the same prompt and student prefix, yielding an aligned pair of next-token predictive distributions. Our aim is to organize the information in this pair into effective supervision. For a prompt x x , let q θ q_{	heta} and p T p_{T} denote the student and frozen teacher. A representative reverse-KL OPD objective is 𝒥 OPD ​ ( θ , x ) \displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{OPD}}(	heta;x) = D KL ( q θ ( ⋅ ∣ x ) ∥ p T ( ⋅ ∣ x ) ) \displaystyle=D_{\mathrm{KL}} igl(q_{	heta}(\cdot\mid x)\|p_{T}(\cdot\mid x) igr) (1) = 𝔼 y ∼ q θ ( ⋅ ∣ x ) [ ∑ t = 1 | y | log q θ ​ ( y t ∣ s t ) p T ​ ( y t ∣ s t ) ] , s t = ( x , y < t ) . \displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim q_{	heta}(\cdot\mid x)}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\log rac{q_{	heta}(y_{t}\mid s_{t})}{p_{T}(y_{t}\mid s_{t})}ight],\qquad s_{t}=(x,y_{<t}). Student-sampled log ratios estimate this objective. At iteration k k , write q k = q θ k q_{k}=q_{	heta_{k}} and collect valid response positions 𝒮 k \mathcal{S}_{k} from fresh student rollouts. A token-local surrogate assigns each sampled token a teacher-to-student log-ratio credit: ℒ ^ OPD , k ( θ ) = − 1 N ∑ t ∈ 𝒮 k sg [ A k , t ] log q θ ( y t ∣ s t ) , A k , t = log p T ​ ( y t ∣ s t ) q k ​ ( y t ∣ s t ) , \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{OPD},k}(	heta)=- rac{1}{N}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{S}_{k}}\operatorname{sg}[A_{k,t}]\log q_{	heta}(y_{t}\mid s_{t}),\qquad A_{k,t}=\log rac{p_{T}(y_{t}\mid s_{t})}{q_{k}(y_{t}\mid s_{t})}, (2) where N = | 𝒮 k | N=|\mathcal{S}_{k}| and sg \operatorname{sg} denotes stop-gradient. Prefixes and credits are fixed during differentiation