refix. The matrix records state-level expectations, while the underlying p p and q q retain the candidate-level resolution needed for an update. It gathers common adaptive OPD signals in one object: student entropy is M 11 M_{11} , teacher entropy is M 22 M_{22} , and teacher–student mismatch appears as the off-diagonal excess over the corresponding self-observation. Table 6 in appendix G compares how different methods are described in this framework. 3.3 Source-centered contrasts reveal ranking and coverage Centering each column on its candidate source’s self-observation isolates the difference between the two evaluators: R \displaystyle R = M 21 − M 11 = H ( q , p ) − H ( q ) = D KL ( q ∥ p ) , \displaystyle=M_{21}-M_{11}=H(q,p)-H(q)=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\|p), (4) F \displaystyle F = M 12 − M 22 = H ( p , q ) − H ( p ) = D KL ( p ∥ q ) . \displaystyle=M_{12}-M_{22}=H(p,q)-H(p)=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p\|q). Thus reverse and forward KL arise as centered cross-observations from the student-sourced and teacher-sourced columns, respectively. Student-sourced contrast: rank and refine active support. For reverse KL, the token-level credit A i = log ⁡ ( p i / q i ) A_{i}=\log(p_{i}/q_{i}) compares teacher support with the student’s current allocation. Since A i > A j A_{i}>A_{j} exactly when p i / q i > p j / q j p_{i}/q_{i}>p_{j}/q_{j} , this credit ranks student-supported candidates by relative underallocation. The student-sourced contrast thereby refines the alternatives the student already explores. Its logit gradient is weighted by q i q_{i} , concentrating the correction on candidates that carry student probability. Teacher-sourced contrast: recover underrepresented support. Forward KL evaluates teacher-sourced candidates under the student. Its logit gradient q − p q-p directly reallocates mass toward teacher-supported alternatives, including those with very small student probability. This coverage correction allocates student capacity across the teacher-supported set ( Gu et al., 2024 ) . In our full-vocabulary construction, the teacher-sourced gradient is evaluated directly at the visited prefix, yielding the exact candidate-level correction from the aligned distributions. From complementary contrasts to a coordination problem. The matrix thus provides both statistics describing the current state and contrasts specifying candidate-level corrections. Existing entropy gates, token filters, and mismatch criteria can be interpreted as selecting or weighting particular regions of this object ( Ko et al., 2026 ; Jin et al., 2026 ; Lee et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026 ; Yu et al., 2026 ) . Together, the source-centered contrasts invite a geometric account of when their ranking/refinement and coverage gradients share a descent direction. Section 4 establishes this compatibility; Section 5 uses self-observation, compatibility, and discrepancy to determine preference, direction, and magnitude. 4 Theory: From Observations to Compatible Gradients The matrix view yields two source-centered corrections whose candidate-level gradients can be studied together. Their ranking/refinement and coverage directions admit common descent, with the observation matrix providing an explicit certificate of compatibility. 4.1 From observation entries to candidate-level gradients For current-state coefficients a s , b s a_{s},b_{s} , define the local matrix loss ℓ s ​ ( θ ) \displaystyle\ell_{s}(	heta) = ⟨ sg ⁡ [ ( − a s b s a s − b s ) ] , M θ ​ ( s ) ⟩ F \displaystyle=\left\langle\operatorname{sg}\!\left[ egin{pmatrix}-a_{s}&b_{s}\ a_{s}&-b_{s}\end{pmatrix}ight],M_{	heta}(s)ightangle_{F} (5) = sg ⁡ [ a s ] ​ [ H ⁡ ( q θ , p ) − H ⁡ ( q θ ) ] + sg ⁡ [ b s ] ​ [ H ⁡ ( p , q θ ) − H ⁡ ( p ) ] . \displaystyle=\operatorname{sg}[a_{s}] igl[H(q_{	heta},p)-H(q_{	heta}) igr]+\operatorname{sg}[b_{s}] igl[H(p,q_{	heta})-H(p) igr]. Writing the coefficient matrix as W s W_{s} , its columns preserve each candidate source’s self-observation as a baseline. The resulting contrast