ts recover the complementary ranking and coverage corrections of reverse and forward KL. The observation–gradient identity establishes their local compatibility, while observation-conditioned coordination combines them in a bounded update that retains a correction for teacher-supported candidates with low student probability. Experiments across non-thinking, thinking, and base-initialized students show consistent gains in mathematical reasoning. Scientific question answering and code generation further assess transfer beyond the mathematical training domain. AI use statement Generative AI tools were used solely for limited assistance with language polishing, formatting, routine coding, and debugging. The research, analysis, interpretation of results, and preparation of the final content were carried out by the human authors. All results and citations were independently verified by the authors, who take full responsibility for the work. Ethics Statement This work studies on-policy distillation using publicly available datasets and pre-trained open-source models, and all experiments follow the licenses of the corresponding resources. Because distillation can transfer characteristics of the teacher into the student, downstream use should include task-appropriate evaluation of factuality, bias, and safety together with standard deployment safeguards. Reproducibility statement Full proofs appear in Appendices. Appendix H describes the gradient implementation, and Appendix I provides training and sampling hyperparameters, evaluation protocols, and controlled comparisons. References Agarwal et al. (2024a) R. Agarwal, N. Vieillard, Y. Zhou, P. Stanczyk, S. Ramos Garea, M. Geist, and O. Bachem On-policy distillation of language models: learning from self-generated mistakes . In The Twelfth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) , Cited by: §6.1 . Agarwal et al. (2024b) R. Agarwal, N. Vieillard, Y. Zhou, P. Stanczyk, S. Ramos, M. Geist, and O. Bachem On-P